llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232, applied after
123): at one token, the Hadamard transforms that a q8_0 KV cache adds
around attention run inside the kernels next to them: the rope of q and k,
the set_rows of k and v into the cache, and the sigmoid gate of the
attention output.
Generic (all GPUs): not gfx803-specific code; measured on gfx803.

WHY
---
Single-token decode on an RX 590 pays for every dispatch as well as for the
work in it: each one costs about 4.3 us of command-processor time however
small the kernel, a 2.65 us floor plus 1.65 us for the vector-L1 invalidate
that every dispatch needs on gfx8 (docs/FINDINGS.md, "A kernel can read
stale data from the vector L1 across dispatches").

With a quantized KV cache llama.cpp rotates q, k and v by a Hadamard matrix
before attention and rotates the attention output back
(llama_mul_mat_hadamard, a MUL_MAT that the CUDA backend runs as the fwht
kernel). In a Qwen3.5/3.6 attention layer at one token that is four small
kernels, each sitting next to another small kernel that touches the same
rows: rope(q) -> fwht; rope(k) -> fwht -> set_rows(k) into the q8_0 cache;
fwht(v) -> set_rows(v); flash attention -> fwht -> sigmoid gate multiply.
None moves more than 16 KiB at one token on Qwen3.6 35B-A3B, so they are
almost all dispatch cost: 50 dispatches per token fewer on Qwen3.6 35B-A3B
(10 attention layers: 1 for q, 2 for k, 1 for v, 1 for the output).

Measured on an RX 590 (Qwen3.6 35B-A3B Q4_K_M, every expert in system RAM,
llama-bench -ncmoe 99, q8_0 KV cache): taken 40 times (10 at each of the
four sites) per decode token, as expected; llama-perplexity at one token per
batch gives per-chunk values and summary lines equal to the build without
the patch, and greedy output (128 tokens) is byte-identical. 123-125
together: tg128 26.33 / 26.26 / 26.31 -> 26.34 / 26.67 / 26.48 tok/s in
three alternating rounds (+0.7%).

WHAT
----
ggml_cuda_try_deferred_fusion also calls three matchers.

ggml_cuda_match_rope_fwht, at a ROPE that ggml_cuda_op_rope sends to
rope_multi (MROPE or IMROPE, f32, contiguous output): the rope is read only
by same-bytes views (offset 0, contiguous, all elements) that end in the
Hadamard MUL_MAT (ggml_cuda_op_mul_mat_use_fwht) over rows of the head size
(64 to 512), with only views in between. When the transform's result is in
turn read only through same-bytes views by a q8_0 SET_ROWS with I64 row ids
(K), that store is part of the match too. rope_multi_fwht runs one row per
warp in fwht_cuda's launch shape: each lane computes rope_multi's value for
its elements (a copy outside the rotated window, otherwise the rotation of
its pair with rope_multi's theta, sections and rope_yarn code), scales it as
fwht_cuda does, runs fwht_cuda's butterfly network (ggml_cuda_fwht_row) and
writes the transformed row, or for K quantizes it into the cache row of its
token's row id (ggml_cuda_quantize_q8_0_lanes, quantize_f32_q8_0_block over
32 lanes). The transform's output may be the rope's input itself (an exact
alias: a warp reads its whole row before it writes it); any other overlap
refuses.

ggml_cuda_match_fwht_set_rows, at a q8_0 SET_ROWS whose source is, through
same-bytes views read only along the chain, a Hadamard MUL_MAT within 48
nodes before it in the graph being evaluated (V, and K when its rope was not
taken): the transform is skipped when the loop reaches it (the match
depends only on the graph and the tensors' memory, so both nodes decide the
same way), and fwht_set_rows_q8_0_cuda transforms the rows of its input and
quantizes them into the cache. Nothing between the two nodes may write the
transform's input; graph_optimize keeps that input allocated until the
SET_ROWS.

ggml_cuda_match_attn_gate, at a SIGMOID -> MUL pair that the fused
unary * mul would take, where the MUL's other operand is a same-bytes view
of a Hadamard MUL_MAT within 48 nodes before it (the attention output
rotated back): the transform is skipped when the loop reaches it, and
fwht_sigmoid_mul_f32 transforms the attention output rows and multiplies by
op_sigmoid of the gate as unary_gated_op_kernel does. Nothing in between may
write the transform's input; the MUL's output may be that input or the gate
itself (exact aliases), nothing else; graph_optimize keeps the input
allocated until the MUL.

All three take one token only (the rope's and the SET_ROWS's token count, a
one-row gate); prompt batches and everything else run as before, and
GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_FUSION=1 turns this off with the other fusions.

Numerics are unchanged: each value is computed by the expressions of the
kernels it replaces, in the same order. The transform is fwht_cuda's
scale and butterfly network on the same lanes; rope values are rope_multi's
expressions; the gate is unary_gated_op_kernel's op_sigmoid(x) * g; the q8_0
store is quantize_f32_q8_0_block's amax, d = amax / 127, id = 1 / d and
roundf(x * id), where amax is a maximum (exact in any order) started at
fmaxf(0, |x|) per lane so that NaN inputs give the serial loop's result.
On gfx803 the fused kernels' floating-point instructions are those of the
kernels they replace: rope_multi_fwht<64> = rope_multi plus fwht_cuda<64>
(the same counts of every mnemonic, the sequences differing only in
scheduling), fwht_set_rows_q8_0_cuda = fwht_cuda plus
k_set_rows_quant<q8_0>'s per-value sequence (v_max for amax, the same two
division expansions, v_mul, the roundf expansion, the conversions),
fwht_sigmoid_mul_f32 = fwht_cuda plus unary_gated_op_kernel<op_sigmoid>.
In the wider instantiations the compiler shares the parts of the rope code
that do not depend on the element (the logarithm of the frequency base);
contraction on gfx803 forms only v_mad/v_mac, which round like the separate
multiply and add, and v_fma appears only inside the library's division and
transcendental expansions, as in rope_multi.

